### Framework config — device/instance-specific, not committed ###
# demoqa.properties / saucedemo.properties ARE tracked on purpose (no
# secrets, just public URL/timeout values). mobile.properties is different:
# it holds a real device serial/IP (mobile.device.name) and possibly a
# local .apk/.ipa path, so it's excluded here instead of relying on nobody
# ever running `git add -A` before it's created. Keep only the .example
# template tracked, same pattern as _TEMPLATE.properties.example.
src/test/resources/config/mobile.properties

### Self-healing locator cache — deliberately lives outside target/ so ###
### `mvn clean` doesn't wipe it (see LocatorRepository.java /           ###
### global.properties self-healing.repository.path); Jenkins caches it  ###
### into $JENKINS_HOME between builds instead, so it must never be      ###
### committed to git.                                                   ###
self-healing-data/

### Android SDK / emulator / Appium — all three CI pipelines (Jenkins,  ###
### GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) install/cache these at the workspace     ###
### root, and GitLab's `cache:` config even persists .android-sdk/      ###
### across runs the same way — none of this is under target/, so       ###
### without these entries a local mobile run (or a debugging run of    ###
### the CI steps outside CI) leaves them untracked and one `git add -A`###
### away from being committed.                                         ###
.android-sdk/
cmdline-tools.zip
# emulator.log / appium.log already caught by the *.log rule above.

### ReportPortal client-java launch synchronization files ###
# Created at the project root (not under target/) by the ReportPortal
# TestNG agent itself whenever reportportal.enable=true and the run's
# parallel forks/threads need to coordinate onto one shared Launch ID —
# reportportal.sync holds that ID, reportportal.lock is the file lock used
# while handing it off. Purely a runtime byproduct of a live RP run, never
# meant to be committed.
reportportal.lock
reportportal.sync

### Allure CLI / report output generated OUTSIDE target/ ###
# Jenkins' Allure plugin writes its generated report to <workspace>/allure-report
# (not target/allure-report — see Jenkinsfile's `allure([...])` post step), and
# GitLab CI's Pages job downloads the Allure CLI tarball and generates into
# public/ at the project root (see .gitlab-ci.yml allure-report job). Both are
# root-level artifacts distinct from the target/allure-report/ path GitHub/GitLab
# test jobs use (already covered by target/ above).
/allure-report/
/allure-2.27.0.tgz
/allure-cli/
/public/

### Python bytecode cache for the .github/workflows/scripts/*.py helpers ###
# Not previously ignored — found untracked (not yet committed, but one
# `git add .` away from being accidentally checked in) under
# .github/workflows/scripts/__pycache__/ during a repo-wide audit.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
docs/obsidian-vault/.obsidian/

# Local secrets (e.g. DASHBOARD_PASSWORD) — never commit (.env.example is the tracked template)
.env
*.env

### Automation Console (Scripts/dashboard) runtime state — never commit ###
# users.json holds per-user PBKDF2 hashes (auth/users.py says it is gitignored, but nothing
# ignored it); secrets.enc.json is the encrypted login/token store (auth/secrets_store.py);
# data/ holds saved specs and run workspaces (spec.json, properties.generated, run.log) which
# can embed internal URLs and test credentials. Only the .gitkeep placeholder stays tracked.
Scripts/dashboard/auth/users.json
Scripts/dashboard/auth/secrets.enc.json
Scripts/dashboard/data/*
!Scripts/dashboard/data/templates/
Scripts/dashboard/data/templates/*
!Scripts/dashboard/data/templates/.gitkeep
